                  "theoryContent": "### Political & Cultural Synthesis of Andhra Dynasties\n\nThe history of Andhra Pradesh exhibits a continuous evolution of socio-political consciousness, agrarian expansion, maritime commerce, and temple architecture.\n\n#### 1. The Satavahanas (Circa 2nd Century BCE - 3rd Century CE)\n* **Foundational Ruler:** Simukha; capital established at Kotilingala / Dhanyakataka (Amaravati).\n* **Greatest Monarch:** **Gautamiputra Satakarni** (Nasik Inscription of Gautami Balasri details his titles: *Trisamudrapitapoyavahana* and *Kshatriyadarpamana-mardana*).\n* **Cultural Contributions:** \n  * Prakrit language patronage (King Hala authored *Gathasaptasati*).\n  * Amaravati school of Buddhist art flourished with white marble reliefs depicting Jataka tales.\n\n#### 2. The Eastern Chalukyas of Vengi (624 CE - 1075 CE)\n* Founded by Kubja Vishnuvardhana (brother of Pulakeshin II).\n* Major significance: **Birth of Telugu Literature** through Rajaraja Narendra's court poet **Nannaya Bhattaraka**, who initiated the *Andhra Mahabharatam*.\n\n#### 3. The Vijayanagara Empire (1336 CE - 1646 CE)\n* Founded on the banks of Tungabhadra by Harihara I & Bukka I.\n* Golden Age under **Sri Krishnadevaraya** (1509–1529 CE), author of *Amuktamalyada* and patron of the *Ashtadiggajas*.",
