                  "theoryContent": "### Constitutional Architecture & Centre-State Dynamics\n\nThe Indian Constitution provides a quasi-federal framework with a unitary bias under extraordinary circumstances (K.C. Wheare).\n\n#### 1. The Basic Structure Doctrine\n* Propounded in the landmark **Kesavananda Bharati v. State of Kerala (1973)**.\n* Parliament can amend any part of the Constitution under Article 368, but cannot alter its basic structure (Supremacy of Constitution, Rule of Law, Judicial Review, Secularism, Federalism).\n\n#### 2. Federal Balancing Mechanisms\n* **Article 263:** Inter-State Council for resolving disputes between States and Union.\n* **Article 280:** Finance Commission for vertical and horizontal devolution of central divisible pool taxes.\n* **Sarkaria Commission (1983):** Recommended rare use of Article 356 (President Rule) and consultation with Chief Ministers before appointing Governors.",
